    {
      "dom_id": "question-116738",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273315/assets/f576679c6728b8ba9fe9e44a42fa36f176697df796ee0a6cc72a9c545c067edf.png)\n\nA snowball is rolling down a snow-covered hillside so that its volume increases at a rate of $40\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is the radius of the snowball increasing at the moment when the radius is $2.5\\,\\text{in}$ in length?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=2.5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi $ and $r=2.5,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (2.5)^{2} \\\\ 40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 25\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the snowball is increasing at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $2.5\\,\\text{in}.$",
